    "lawCode": "Amd §1, Chap 21 of 1962",
    "lawSection": "Local Emergency Housing Rent Control Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6916A\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the local emergency housing rent control act, in\nrelation to the date in calendar year 2022 when the local legislative\nbody of a city having a population of one million or more may determine\nthe continuation of the emergency; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon the expiration thereof\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill amends subdivision 3 of section 1 of chapter 21\nof the laws of 1962, constituting the local emergency housing rent\ncontrol act, by providing that, to ensure sufficient time to complete\nthe requisite housing survey in the context of the COVID-19 pandemic,\nthe latest date by which a local legislative body may make a determi-\nnation regarding the existence of a public emergency requiring the regu-\nlation and control of residential rents and evictions within cities\nhaving a population of one million or more in calendar year 2022 is\npostponed to July 1, 2022.\n\nSection two of the bill provides that it would take effect immediately\nand expire and be deemed repealed on December 31, 2023, provided that\nsuch expiration and repeal will not affect the validity of any determi-\nnation consistent with this act made in calendar year 2022.\n\n \nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nRent stabilization and control are critical protections for the City of\nNew York (the City), which has had a continued housing crisis since at\nleast the 1960s. Under existing law, in cities with a population of one\nmillion or more, any determination as to the continuation of an ongoing\nhousing emergency shall be made at least once every three to four years\nfollowing a survey that such city shall cause to be made of the supply\nof housing accommodations within such city, the condition of such accom-\nmodations and the need for continuing the regulation and control of\nresidential rents and evictions within such city. The City has histor-\nically made this determination using the New York City Housing and\nVacancy Survey (NYCHVS), which is conducted by the United States Census\nBureau (the Census Bureau) on behalf of the City.\n\nThe most recent NYCHVS was conducted in 2017, and the City determination\nof housing emergency was made in 2018. Because the Census Bureau\nconducted the decennial census in 2020 and would not have been able to\ncomplete the NYCHVS that year, the State Legislature extended the dead-\nline for the City to make its next determination of emergency and future\nsuch determinations that would compete with the decennial census. The\nNew York City Council (City Council) then modified the expiration date\nof the Rent Stabilization Law of 1969, giving the City until April 2022\nto complete the NYCHVS, deliver the select initial findings and deter-\nmine whether an emergency still exists.\n\nThe COVID-19 pandemic caused significant disruptions to normal business\noperations across New York City during much of 2020 and well into the\nfirst four months of 2021. The pandemic contributed to frustration of\nthe Census Bureau's recruitment and hiring of field staff, slowing the\nrollout of the 2021 NYCHVS field period, which began in February 2021.\nIn light of these delays, the Census Bureau requires additional time to\ncomplete its normal fieldwork. This bill would postpone the date by\nwhich the City may make a determination as to the continuation of the\nhousing emergency from April 1, 2022 until July 1, 2022 to give the\nCensus Bureau three additional months to complete field work.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable consider-\nation of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
